{"text": "Copyright 1996 The Dayton Daily News\n\u2018The Dayton Daily News\n\nJanuary 27, 1996, SATURDAY, CITY EDITION\nSECTION: OPED PAGE, Pg. 11A\nLENG?\n\n804 words\nHEADLINE: TEEN-SMOKING CRISIS IS REALLY OVERBLOWN\nBYLINE: Lowell Gallaway\n\nBopy:\nIt is a familiar tactic on the part of power-hungry bureaucrats and\npoliticians: Create a crisis - preferably one that centers about the welfare of\nthe aged, the poor or children = and then announce that the government is ready\nto march\u2019 onto the scene and solve it. Of course, almost always, the government\nsolution involves more regulation and interference in the lives of American\ncitizens. However, the regulators say that is the price we must pay for social\nprogress.\n\n\u2018The latest example of this sort is the \"teen-age smoking epidemic\" proclaimed\nby President Bill Clinton and the Food and Drug Administration. Whenever I hear\nsomething like that, I reach for my copy of the Statistical Abstract of the\nUnited States. Most of the time I can locate sone useful numbers.\n\n\u2018This was no exception, although it was a surprise. What I found was a\npronounced general downward trend in the percentage of young people (ages 12-17)\n\u00a5ho were current users of cigarettes. In 1974, 25 percent of this group were\nClassed as current users of cigarettes. By 1979 this was down to 12.1 percent.\nBut it was back up to 15.3 percent in i985 ~ and then down again, to 9.6 percent\nin 1992. To be sure, there is some evidence of increases over the past two\nyears, but that is not uncommon in this data series. What we need to look at is\nthe longer-term decline in cigarette smoking among the young. Let's not be\nsidetracked by the short-term, year-to-year variations.\n\nWhat these data indicate is that the magnitude of the teen-smoking issue has\nbeen exaggerated. Why? Two reasons cone quickly to mind. _\n\nFirst, the very concept of governnent regulation is being questioned most\nvigorously in today's political climate. Agencies such as the FDA, whose record\nin delaying the introduction of new drugs and therapeutic devices\u2019has cone under\nsubstantial criticism, feel themselves threatened. They need new crusades and\nnew initiatives to justify their continued existence, to help them avoid the\nbudget-cutter's ax in this changing political environment.\n\nFor the FDA, cigarette smoking among the young presents a marvelous\nopportunity to execute the standard bureaucratic end run. Already, it has\nproposed a host of new regulations, including the banning of vending machines\nand onerous restrictions on cigarette advertising and monitoring the nature and\ncontent of T-shirt logos and graffiti. It is a regulator's dream. After all, who\nwill argue with attempting to \"protect\" the health of children?\n\nfoe 2061853156", "words": [{"text": "Copyright", "x0": 257, "y0": 166, "x1": 324, "y1": 178, "conf": 94.0}, {"text": "1996", "x0": 333, "y0": 167, "x1": 361, "y1": 175, "conf": 95.0}, {"text": "The", "x0": 370, "y0": 166, "x1": 392, "y1": 175, "conf": 96.0}, {"text": "Dayton", "x0": 400, "y0": 167, "x1": 445, "y1": 177, "conf": 96.0}, {"text": "Daily", "x0": 453, "y0": 166, "x1": 490, "y1": 178, "conf": 96.0}, {"text": "News", "x0": 498, "y0": 167, "x1": 527, "y1": 175, "conf": 92.0}, {"text": "\u2018The", "x0": 325, "y0": 179, "x1": 347, "y1": 188, "conf": 89.0}, {"text": "Dayton", "x0": 355, "y0": 179, "x1": 400, "y1": 190, "conf": 95.0}, {"text": "Daily", "x0": 408, "y0": 178, "x1": 445, "y1": 190, "conf": 96.0}, {"text": "News", "x0": 452, "y0": 180, "x1": 482, "y1": 187, "conf": 96.0}, {"text": "January", "x0": 249, "y0": 204, "x1": 302, "y1": 215, "conf": 95.0}, {"text": "27,", "x0": 310, "y0": 204, "x1": 330, "y1": 214, "conf": 96.0}, {"text": "1996,", "x0": 341, "y0": 204, "x1": 375, "y1": 214, "conf": 96.0}, {"text": "SATURDAY,", "x0": 385, "y0": 204, "x1": 450, "y1": 214, "conf": 96.0}, {"text": "CITY", "x0": 468, "y0": 204, "x1": 497, "y1": 213, "conf": 82.0}, {